"titre": "Loneliness in an Increasingly Connected World",
"titre2": "Exercice b003 (Narration / Récit)",
"texte": [
"As Clara stood on the balcony of her twentieth-floor apartment, the city stretched out beneath her like a luminous circuit board. Inside, her smartphone chimed incessantly, alerting her to dozens of reactions on a photo she had posted an hour earlier. On paper, she was the epitome of social integration: she boasted over two thousand online followers, belonged to six active messaging groups, and attended virtual networking events weekly. Nevertheless, an acute, hollow silence enveloped her living room.",
"Over the past three years, her genuine friendships had subtly eroded, replaced by effortless yet superficial digital interactions. Whenever she felt overwhelmed, she scrolled through curated feeds of acquaintances smiling from exotic beaches, which only intensified her sense of isolation. Although she was perpetually reachable, she realized she had no one she felt comfortable calling unannounced at ten o'clock on a Tuesday evening.",
"Sociologists frequently refer to this paradox as 'hyper-connected loneliness.' Digital platforms are engineered to simulate intimacy while bypassing the vulnerability that real human bonding requires. A quick emoji reaction offers the illusion of empathy without demanding time, presence, or emotional investment. Watching the distant headlights trace the avenues below, Clara made a quiet resolution. If she wanted to dismantle the invisible wall surrounding her, she would have to log off and risk the awkwardness of face-to-face connection once again."
