"titre": "The Importance of Developing Critical Thinking from an Early Age",
"titre2": "Exercice b014 (Semi-dialogue / Radio Interview)",
"texte": [
"Interviewer: Good morning, listeners. Today on 'Education Today,' we’re joined by cognitive psychologist Dr. Julian Mercer to discuss why teaching critical thinking in primary school has become an urgent priority. Dr. Mercer, many people assume young children are too concrete in their thinking to analyze complex arguments. Why start so early?",
"Dr. Mercer: That assumption underestimates children’s natural curiosity. Between ages six and ten, children constantly ask 'why?'—yet traditional schooling often trains them to stop asking questions and simply memorize 'correct' answers. If we wait until university to introduce critical reasoning, we are attempting to undo years of passive habits. Starting early teaches children that knowledge isn't a static list of rules handed down by authority, but something to be explored, questioned, and verified.",
"Interviewer: Given the overwhelming volume of misinformation circulating on digital platforms today, how does early critical thinking protect young learners?",
"Dr. Mercer: Immensely. Today’s children grow up immersed in algorithms that blur the line between journalism, entertainment, and disguised advertising. Even simple classroom exercises—such as comparing two conflicting accounts of the same event or asking 'Who created this message and why?'—equip students with intellectual immunity. Rather than telling children what to think, we teach them how to evaluate evidence and recognize emotional manipulation.",
"Interviewer: Some parents worry that encouraging children to question everything might make them disrespectful toward teachers or family rules. How do you respond to that concern?",
"Dr. Mercer: That is a common misconception. Critical thinking is not cynicism or rebellion; it is disciplined, respectful inquiry. In fact, children trained in constructive reasoning become better listeners because they learn to distinguish valid arguments from mere prejudice. Ultimately, fostering independent judgment from childhood isn't about undermining authority—it’s about preparing thoughtful, resilient citizens capable of sustaining a healthy democracy."
],
