"titre": "Is Education the Key to a More Equitable Society?",
"titre2": "Exercice b020 (Argumentative Essay / Essai)",
"texte": [
"Since the Enlightenment, progressive thinkers have hailed universal education as the cornerstone of a just and democratic society. According to this enduring ideal, providing every citizen with access to knowledge dismantles inherited privilege, empowers individuals to participate meaningfully in civic life, and enables merit to triumph over social origin. Undoubtedly, mass literacy and expanded access to schooling have historically driven remarkable social mobility, lifted millions out of poverty, and fostered greater gender equality worldwide. Nevertheless, in an era marked by widening economic polarization, we must ask whether education alone can deliver a truly equitable society, or whether expecting schools to single-handedly cure structural injustice places an unrealistic burden on classrooms.",
"Proponents of education as the primary engine of equality point to compelling evidence. Higher educational attainment correlates strongly with increased lifetime earnings, better health outcomes, and greater resilience against economic downturns. Furthermore, inclusive classrooms bring together young people from diverse religious, cultural, and socioeconomic backgrounds, cultivating the mutual empathy and critical citizenship necessary to challenge prejudice. From this perspective, expanding access to high-quality education remains the most powerful lever governments possess to level the playing field.",
"However, sociologists and economists increasingly caution against treating education as a substitute for broader social reform. Even when disadvantaged students earn university degrees, they frequently encounter persistent barriers in the labor market—including unpaid internships that only wealthy families can subsidize, implicit hiring biases, and a lack of influential professional networks. Moreover, as higher education becomes more widespread, 'credential inflation' often occurs: jobs that previously required a secondary diploma now demand a master’s degree, constantly moving the goalpost for working-class youth. In other words, schools cannot easily equalize outcomes if the surrounding economic structure continues to concentrate wealth and opportunity among a narrow elite.",
"Ultimately, education is an indispensable catalyst for social justice, yet it is not a self-sufficient panacea. Schools can unlock human potential and nurture informed citizens, but genuine equity also requires fair labor laws, affordable housing, accessible healthcare, and progressive taxation. Only when educational opportunity is paired with broader economic fairness can society fulfill its promise of equal dignity for all."
