"titre": "Is Work Still a Source of Personal Fulfillment?",
"titre2": "Exercice b029 (Semi-dialogue / Sociological Interview)",
"texte": [
"Interviewer: Good evening and welcome to 'Work & Society.' Following widespread trends like 'quiet quitting' and career downsizing, many observers claim that people no longer look to their jobs for personal meaning. Today we’re joined by organizational sociologist Dr. Elena Rostova. Dr. Rostova, has work lost its power to fulfill us?",
"Dr. Rostova: Thank you. Rather than saying work has lost its meaning, I would argue that workers are rejecting an unhealthy myth—namely, the idea that one’s job must serve as one’s sole identity and spiritual calling. For decades, modern culture encouraged people to 'love what you do,' which subtly pressured employees into accepting excessive hours and stagnant wages in exchange for vague promises of passion. Today’s workforce is simply demanding a healthier, more realistic relationship with employment.",
"Interviewer: Does that mean most workers now view their jobs purely as a financial transaction—exchanging hours for a paycheck?",
"Dr. Rostova: Not entirely. Empirical surveys consistently show that humans still crave purpose, mastery, and social contribution through their labor. When individuals engage in work that exercises their creativity, solves genuine problems, or benefits their community, employment remains a profound source of self-esteem and belonging. However, fulfillment collapses when jobs become overly bureaucratic, micromanaged, or disconnected from tangible human impact—a phenomenon anthropologists call 'alienated labor.'",
"Interviewer: How, then, can organizations restore a sense of fulfillment without exploiting employees' enthusiasm?",
"Dr. Rostova: By focusing on dignity rather than corporate slogans. Meaningful work thrives when employers grant genuine autonomy, recognize contributions fairly, ensure psychological safety, and respect personal boundaries. At the same time, individuals must resist putting all their existential eggs in one professional basket. Ultimately, work can—and should—be a rewarding chapter of human flourishing, provided we remember that it is only one pillar of a rich life alongside family, friendship, civic engagement, and leisure."
