"titre": "Is Prevention More Effective Than the Treatment of Diseases?",
"titre2": "Exercice b055 (Argumentative Essay / Essai)",
"texte": [
"The ancient maxim that 'an ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure' has long been accepted as common sense, yet modern healthcare systems continue to allocate the overwhelming majority of their budgets to treating established illnesses rather than preventing them. Across industrialized nations, typically less than three percent of total health expenditure is dedicated to preventive initiatives, while hospitals absorb vast sums managing advanced cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, and preventable cancers. Given escalating medical costs and aging populations, health economists and physicians are increasingly examining whether shifting priority toward prevention is genuinely more effective—and practical—than focusing on curative treatment.",
"Proponents of preventive medicine present compelling epidemiological and financial arguments. Primary prevention—such as childhood vaccination programs, tobacco taxation, clean air regulations, and public campaigns encouraging regular physical activity and balanced nutrition—averts human suffering before it begins. Economically, preventing a chronic condition costs a fraction of lifelong pharmacological management, emergency hospitalizations, or complex surgeries. Furthermore, secondary prevention through early screening tests detects malignancies or hypertension at stages when interventions are minimally invasive and survival rates are dramatically higher. In other words, prevention preserves both human vitality and the fiscal sustainability of public health systems.",
"Nevertheless, implementing prevention at scale encounters subtle clinical and political obstacles. Unlike curative medicine, whose benefits are immediate and visible when a surgeon saves a patient's life, preventive measures yield invisible statistical outcomes decades later—making them far less politically rewarding for governments operating on short electoral cycles. Moreover, critics note that indiscriminate screening or over-medicalization of healthy individuals can occasionally trigger unnecessary anxiety, false positives, and costly overtreatment. Additionally, encouraging behavioral change remains notoriously difficult when unhealthy ultra-processed foods are heavily marketed and socioeconomically disadvantaged communities lack safe recreational spaces.",
"Ultimately, prevention and treatment are not rival philosophies, but complementary pillars of a resilient healthcare system. While cutting-edge acute care will always remain vital when illness strikes unexpectedly, prioritizing upstream prevention—through equitable public health policies, healthier urban environments, and targeted early screening—offers the most humane and cost-effective path toward extending healthy human life."
