"titre": "Do Medical Advances Raise New Ethical Issues?",
"titre2": "Exercice b059 (Semi-dialogue / Medical Ethics Broadcast)",
"texte": [
"Host: Welcome to 'Modern Medicine Today.' From artificial intelligence diagnosing rare tumors to life-support systems sustaining patients indefinitely, medical innovation is saving lives once considered beyond hope. Yet every breakthrough seems to bring unprecedented moral dilemmas. Today we’re joined by medical ethicist Dr. Julian Thorne. Dr. Thorne, why do modern medical advances generate so many complex ethical questions?",
"Dr. Thorne: Thank you. Simply put, our technological capabilities have outpaced our moral and legal frameworks. For centuries, medicine focused on a straightforward imperative: preserve life whenever possible. Today, however, intensive care technologies can maintain biological functions long after consciousness has irreversibly ceased, forcing families and physicians to agonize over end-of-life decisions, palliative dignity, and the definition of quality of life.",
"Host: Artificial intelligence is also rapidly entering clinical practice—predicting patient outcomes and recommending treatments. What ethical risks accompany algorithmic medicine?",
"Dr. Thorne: Several critical concerns arise. First, if an AI diagnostic model is trained predominantly on medical data from wealthy, homogeneous populations, it may produce inaccurate or biased recommendations for underrepresented ethnic groups. Second, algorithmic decision-making blurs accountability: if an automated system misdiagnoses a patient, does responsibility lie with the physician, the hospital, or the software developer? Finally, over-relying on predictive algorithms risks eroding the empathetic doctor-patient relationship, reducing human suffering to cold statistical probabilities.",
"Host: What about revolutionary therapies like personalized cancer treatments or synthetic organs that cost hundreds of thousands of dollars per patient? How do healthcare systems navigate that challenge?",
"Dr. Thorne: That touches upon distributive justice—arguably the sharpest ethical dilemma in modern healthcare. When medical breakthroughs are priced beyond the reach of public budgets or average citizens, medicine risks splitting into two tiers: miraculous longevity for the ultra-wealthy and basic care for everyone else. Ultimately, medical progress is truly ethical only when guided by transparency, human accountability, and equitable access for all patients."
