"titre": "How Can We Reconcile Scientific Progress with Respect for Human Dignity?",
"titre2": "Exercice b060 (Argumentative Essay / Essai)",
"texte": [
"From the splitting of the atom to the decoding of the human genome and the emergence of autonomous artificial intelligence, modern science has endowed humanity with extraordinary power over both nature and ourselves. Throughout history, scientific inquiry has liberated millions from superstition, backbreaking labor, and premature mortality. Nevertheless, as technological innovation accelerates into intimate domains of human existence—including biometric surveillance, neurotechnology, algorithmic profiling, and genetic engineering—a profound philosophical question arises: how can society foster bold scientific progress without eroding the foundational principle of human dignity?",
"To reconcile innovation with dignity, we must first reject the deterministic fallacy that whatever is technologically possible is automatically desirable or inevitable. Technological tools are never morally neutral; they embed the priorities and values of those who design and deploy them. When biometric facial recognition is used to monitor citizens without consent, or when workplace algorithms track employees' every keystroke to maximize efficiency, scientific ingenuity becomes an instrument of dehumanization—treating human beings as quantifiable data points rather than autonomous moral agents endowed with privacy and free will.",
"On the other hand, halting scientific research out of fear would betray human dignity just as severely. Denying patients access to regenerative medicine, assistive robotics, or clean energy innovations condemns millions to avoidable suffering. Therefore, the solution lies not in stifling curiosity, but in embedding ethical reflection at the very inception of scientific design—a principle known as 'ethics by design.' Rather than treating ethicists, sociologists, and philosophers as bureaucratic obstacles after a technology is already deployed, interdisciplinary teams must evaluate social impacts, consent mechanisms, and human rights safeguards while innovations are still in the laboratory.",
"Furthermore, democratic governance must ensure that the direction of scientific progress reflects collective human values rather than narrow commercial or geopolitical competition. Transparent public consultation, enforceable data protection laws, and international treaties governing high-risk technologies empower citizens to shape their technological future. Ultimately, science achieves its highest purpose not when it demonstrates unbounded technical mastery, but when it remains a humble servant to human freedom, equality, and intrinsic dignity."
