"texte": [
"Until his final year of university, Samuel regarded fiction as an agreeable distraction—something to occupy a rainy Sunday afternoon once serious textbooks in economics and statistics had been closed. Raised in an affluent suburb, he believed that understanding society required mastering quantitative data: unemployment percentages, inflation indices, and demographic charts. Novels, by contrast, seemed merely invented stories divorced from empirical reality. That conviction unraveled completely the autumn he picked up a worn paperback detailing the lives of nineteenth-century textile workers, followed soon after by contemporary novels written by migrant authors.",
"Reading late into the night in his quiet apartment, Samuel experienced what psychologists call 'narrative transportation.' Unlike dry statistical tables that reduced human hardship to abstract digits, literature invited him to inhabit another person’s consciousness from the inside. Through vividly rendered characters, he felt the claustrophobic anxiety of a single mother calculating rent down to the last coin, the quiet humiliation of linguistic exclusion in a foreign land, and the moral compromises forced upon ordinary people by unjust institutions. Suddenly, social issues he had previously analyzed with detached clinical neutrality acquired human faces, voices, and emotional urgency.",
"History offers ample confirmation that Samuel’s awakening was far from unique. From Charles Dickens exposing the cruelty of Victorian workhouses to Harper Lee confronting racial injustice, literature has repeatedly punctured societal complacency by making invisible suffering impossible to ignore. Of course, a novel cannot directly draft legislation or abolish poverty overnight; as critics rightly note, reading about injustice does not automatically translate into political action. Nevertheless, by cultivating radical empathy and challenging inherited prejudices, literature reshapes the moral imagination of readers. When Samuel eventually began his career in urban housing policy, he carried with him a transformed perspective: data could measure the scale of a social problem, yet only human stories could reveal its true weight—and inspire the determination to build a fairer society."
