"Dr. Mensah: Thank you. Framing language preservation as an obstacle to global communication rests on a false choice; human beings are naturally capable of multilingualism. More fundamentally, a language is never merely a neutral tool for exchanging practical information—it is the living archive of a community’s history, ecological knowledge, humor, and worldview. When a local language vanishes, humanity loses irreplaceable insights into medicinal plants, sustainable land stewardship, and unique ways of conceptualizing time and human relationships.",
"Interviewer: What are the primary forces driving young speakers away from their ancestral tongues today?",
