"titre": "Cinema as a Reflection of Social Change",
"titre2": "Exercice b065 (Argumentative Essay / Essai)",
"texte": [
"Ever since the Lumière brothers projected images of factory workers leaving their shifts onto a Parisian screen in 1895, cinema has maintained an intimate, reciprocal relationship with social history. Far from being merely commercial escapism designed to entertain audiences for two hours in darkened theaters, motion pictures serve as vivid cultural barometers—capturing the anxieties, aspirations, and moral transformations of the eras in which they are produced. Yet, examining cinema’s evolution raises an intriguing question: does film merely mirror social changes that have already occurred, or can it actively accelerate cultural progress?",
"On the one hand, historians frequently analyze cinema as a faithful archive of shifting societal norms. Comparing mainstream films from the mid-twentieth century with contemporary productions reveals dramatic evolutions in gender roles, family structures, labor relations, and attitudes toward authority. During periods of economic depression or geopolitical tension, cinema has consistently channeled collective fears—whether through gritty social realism exposing working-class hardship or science-fiction allegories reflecting anxieties over nuclear annihilation and technological surveillance. In this sense, filmmakers inevitably absorb the cultural climate surrounding them, embedding contemporary values and blind spots directly into their visual storytelling.",
"On the other hand, bold cinema has repeatedly functioned as a catalyst that challenges entrenched taboos and expands public empathy. By placing marginalized protagonists at the center of compelling narratives, visionary directors have humanized experiences long ignored or caricatured by mainstream society—from civil rights struggles and LGBTQ+ visibility to environmental activism and neurodiversity. When audiences emotionally invest in characters whose backgrounds differ vastly from their own, abstract political debates transform into deeply personal human connections, often softening prejudices faster than formal lectures or legislative campaigns.",
"Nevertheless, cinema’s power to reflect or inspire change remains constrained by economic and political realities. Because major studio productions require immense financial backing, commercial risk-aversion frequently favors formulaic sequels over daring social critique, while state censorship in authoritarian regimes continues to silence dissident filmmakers. Ultimately, cinema is neither a passive mirror nor an omnipotent revolutionary weapon; rather, it is a dynamic cultural conversation—one that both documents where society stands today and illuminates what it might courageously become tomorrow."
