    "texte": [
      "Members of the City Council, cultural directors, artists, and fellow citizens,",
      "Whenever municipal budgets tighten during periods of economic inflation, a familiar refrain echoes through council chambers: funding for public libraries, municipal theaters, museums, and community music schools must be slashed first because culture is an 'expendable luxury' that can wait until basic material priorities are secured. At first glance, prioritizing paved roads, healthcare clinics, and housing over symphonies or art exhibitions appears impeccably pragmatic. Nevertheless, reducing culture to a decorative ornament reserved for affluent times reflects a profound misunderstanding of what sustains human dignity and social cohesion.",
      "On the one hand, nobody would dispute that physical survival requires food, shelter, and medical care before poetry or painting. When families struggle to afford groceries or heating, spending public funds on extravagant prestige monuments understandably provokes frustration. Consequently, cultural institutions must remain financially accountable and avoid elitism, ensuring that public subsidies serve ordinary residents rather than a narrow circle of wealthy patrons.",
      "On the other hand, human beings do not live by biological sustenance alone; we are meaning-making creatures who rely on shared stories, music, literature, and visual arts to process grief, celebrate milestones, and imagine better futures. During recent global lockdowns, when physical movement was restricted, people did not turn solely to emergency manuals—they turned instinctively to books, films, songs, and virtual concerts to preserve their mental resilience. Furthermore, accessible neighborhood cultural centers act as vital civic anchors, bringing together residents across generational and socioeconomic divides while stimulating local tourism and creative employment.",
      "Ultimately, treating culture as a luxury guarantees that artistic expression and critical thought become the exclusive privilege of those who can afford private tickets and expensive books. Investing in free, inclusive public culture is not frivolous extravagance; rather, it is an essential investment in collective mental health, democratic vitality, and our shared humanity. Thank you."
