"titre2": "Exercice b068 (Narration / Récit)",
"texte": [
"As a secondary school counselor in Manchester, Elena had spent fifteen years observing how adolescent role models evolved. When she first began her career, teenagers admired distant pop stars or athletes glimpsed occasionally on television screens and magazine covers. By the time fourteen-year-old Maya sat nervously across from Elena’s desk one rainy Tuesday morning, however, celebrity culture had undergone a radical transformation. Through smartphones, influencers, musicians, and online creators streamed directly into teenagers’ bedrooms twenty-four hours a day, cultivating an intense illusion of personal friendship that psychologists term 'parasocial intimacy.'",
"Maya’s teachers had referred her to Elena after noticing a sudden decline in her grades, persistent exhaustion, and growing anxiety around her physical appearance. During their conversation, Maya confessed that she spent nearly five hours every evening scrolling through the curated feeds of her favorite lifestyle celebrities. Convinced that these figures were effortlessly wealthy, perpetually glamorous, and flawless, Maya had begun skipping meals to mimic their diet routines and pressuring her parents to buy expensive skincare products endorsed in sponsored posts. She was entirely unaware that those seemingly candid snapshots were carefully staged using professional lighting, digital retouching, and lucrative commercial contracts.",
"Elena knew that celebrity influence was not inherently destructive; indeed, when prominent athletes or artists speak candidly about mental health, champion environmental causes, or encourage civic participation, they can inspire remarkable generosity and self-acceptance among youth. Nevertheless, when commercialized fame promotes unattainable beauty standards, impulsive materialism, or reckless viral stunts, impressionable adolescents often pay a steep psychological price. Over the following weeks, Elena organized media-literacy workshops where students deconstructed sponsored content, analyzed photo-editing techniques, and discussed real-life community mentors—teachers, coaches, and family members—whose everyday integrity offered far healthier models of success. Watching Maya gradually regain her confidence and reconnect with real-world friendships, Elena reflected that while young people will always look up to cultural icons, equipping them with critical awareness ensures they admire celebrities without sacrificing their own self-worth."
